agent-power-manager (0.0.5) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * An expert (agent) resource may now carry its own exclusive skills,
    plugins and MCP servers alongside agent.yaml. The agent root closed set
    grows from "agent.yaml plus optional assets/" to also allow optional
    skills/, plugins/ and mcp/, so an agent ships as one archive with the
    capabilities it needs instead of asking the user to install each
    companion power separately and hoping none is missed. See ADR-046.
  * Sub-resources stay bare directories and files inside the same
    deterministic tar.gz: skills/<name>/ is a complete skill,
    plugins/<name>/ is a complete APC plugin, mcp/<name>.json is a complete
    MCP resource. No nested archives -- the validators reject archive
    extensions and magic, and nesting would add a packing layer on upload, an
    extraction layer on install, and break "the archive root is the resource
    root". Upload, download, extraction, cache, install and recovery all keep
    working through the existing chain; only the root closed set widens.
  * Identity comes from the sub-resource's own content, not its directory
    name: the skill's SKILL.md frontmatter, the plugin's apc-plugin.json, the
    MCP resource's name and version. A standalone skill, plugin or MCP
    directory can therefore be copied in unchanged. Directory names are just
    containers and only have to be non-empty and not dot-prefixed.
  * Each sub-resource must be self-consistent. A skill missing SKILL.md or
    skill-card.md, a plugin missing apc-plugin.json or with a declared skills/
    subtree that does not match, or an MCP file that does not decode fails the
    whole resource -- a half-finished bundle is never installed. Identities are
    unique within a type; the same name may appear across types (skill demo and
    MCP demo can coexist).
  * Sub-resources reuse the authoritative validation of their own type rather
    than a second set of rules: skills go through the new internal/skillartifact
    package (the same implementation used by standalone skills and plugin-
    embedded skills), plugins through pluginartifact's manifest and closed-set
    checks, MCP through domain.DecodeMCPResourceJSON plus ValidateSubmit.
  * Skill format validation moved from internal/submission down into the leaf
    package internal/skillartifact. submission depends on agentartifact, and
    agentartifact now validates skill subtrees too, so without the extraction
    the dependency would cycle. submission keeps thin delegating wrappers and
    bridges skillartifact.ErrInvalid into its own ErrInvalid with errors.Join,
    so existing errors.Is assertions and error messages are unchanged.
  * Every existing safety rule still applies inside sub-resources: path
    canonicalisation, duplicate paths, symlinks and special files, the
    executable bit, nested archives, 10 MiB per file, 100 MiB total, depth 16
    and 255-byte paths. Sub-resources are not a way around them.
  * Installed layout does not move: skills/, plugins/ and mcp/ stay inside
    agents/<name>/. APM does not split them into Bibit's global directories,
    because that needs the runtime's discovery rules and this repository
    cannot confirm them. The agent.yaml skills and mcps fields are still passed
    through unchecked; requiring them to match the directories would turn
    existing valid resources invalid, so consistency is recorded as a known
    limitation for a later ADR.
  * Tests cover the new leaf package, the widened root closed set, incomplete
    and duplicate sub-resources, cross-type names, safety rules inside
    subtrees, validate/submit accepting and packing sub-resources into one
    archive, byte-identical repacking, and the adapter landing sub-resources
    inside agents/<name>/ while rejecting an incomplete bundle before publish.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:10:00 +0800


agent-power-manager (0.0.4) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * `apm upgrade` now asks before it changes anything, like `apt upgrade`:
    once the resources that need upgrading are filtered, it lists them and
    asks "Do you want to continue? [Y/n]". Enter or y/yes proceeds, n/no
    aborts with no changes and a non-zero exit so scripts can tell that
    nothing was upgraded. EOF counts as no -- an ended input stream is never
    treated as consent. The question is skipped when nothing needs upgrading.
  * New -y/--yes skips the question. The confirmation happens inside the lock
    and after planning, because ADR-045 requires the plan and the execution to
    share one SnapshotLocked result; asking outside the lock would let the
    catalog drift so that the set you confirmed is not the set that runs. The
    cost is that the global lock is held while the prompt waits, which matches
    apt holding its own lock while it prompts.
  * The gate fails closed. Without --yes, `--json` (a machine contract, so it
    never prompts) or a stdin that is not a real terminal is refused before
    the lifecycle is created, the lock taken or the catalog synced, and the
    error says to pass --yes. This is a deliberate breaking change:
    `apm upgrade --json` now requires -y. --dry-run changes nothing, so it
    never requires -y.
  * Interactivity is decided by isatty, not by os.ModeCharDevice: /dev/null
    and /dev/zero are character devices too, so the old-style check would send
    `apm upgrade < /dev/null` and CI pipes into a prompt that can never be
    answered. Unix uses a termios ioctl and Windows uses GetConsoleMode.
  * Text output no longer prints an up-to-date line per resource; in a batch
    they bury the entries that matter. A trailing summary line carries the
    counts instead (for example "2 upgraded, 3 up-to-date, 1 skipped.", or
    "nothing to upgrade"). --json still carries every record including
    up-to-date, since machine consumers need to total things up themselves,
    and adds no text summary.
  * Long operations now report stage progress on stderr -- reading-installed,
    syncing-catalog, planning, and a [i/n] line per upgrade -- so the command
    does not look hung. This cannot use slog.Info: info-level logs route to
    stdout by ADR-004 and would corrupt the --json contract, so progress goes
    to stderr and is suppressed under --json. Progress is diagnostic rather
    than a business result, which is why it may be emitted inside the lock;
    result lines are still rendered only after a successful Release.
  * CLIEntry gains SetStdin so the confirmation reads from a deterministic
    source in tests instead of the process-wide os.Stdin. See ADR-045.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:05:00 +0800


agent-power-manager (0.0.3) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Add `apm upgrade`: reconcile every installed power against one catalog
    snapshot and upgrade them all. It takes no positional argument -- single
    resource upgrades stay on `apm install <power>[=<version>]` -- and
    defaults to every installed agent, ordered by agent ID.
  * Upgrade never downgrades. When the catalog latest is behind the local
    version the entry is skipped with a warning (skip=newer-local), and there
    is no --allow-downgrade. An entry whose name has no catalog candidate at
    all is skipped as not-in-catalog instead of failing the whole run.
  * A single failure is reported as `failed` and the remaining entries keep
    going, unlike install's fail-fast. The one condition that does stop the
    run is a residual transaction journal, because a next transaction cannot
    be created safely; the rest is reported as `unexecuted` with a hint to
    run `apm fix`.
  * The outcome vocabulary is deliberately separate from install's:
    installed/upgraded/downgraded/planned/up-to-date/skipped/failed/
    unexecuted. `unchanged` could not be reused because it conflates "the
    explicit request is already satisfied" with "the plan decided to do
    nothing", and `planned` keeps --dry-run from claiming a change happened.
    Install results are mapped faithfully, so a repair after a manually
    deleted target reports `installed` rather than posing as `upgraded`.
  * MCP replacement needs the previous version's catalog entry for rollback,
    so a withdrawn local MCP version is skipped; the other three types do not
    depend on it and still upgrade. --dry-run writes nothing at all, and an
    empty reconciliation (nothing installed) returns before any catalog
    access, so it needs no network.
  * --json is a machine contract: records always carry agent, type, name,
    current_version, target_version, outcome and skip, while warnings and
    skip reasons stay on stderr. See ADR-045 and Feature 032.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:20:00 +0800


agent-power-manager (0.0.2) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Rename the Expert power type locally. APC still calls it "agent" on the wire
    (metadata type, catalog group, content path), and the disk layout keeps
    agents/<name> and agent.yaml, so all of that is translated at the HTTP
    boundary in internal/cloud/remote/type_vocabulary.go instead of being
    renamed out from under APC, Bibit and already-published content.
  * The CLI value is now --type=expert. --type=agent is still accepted and is
    rewritten to expert on the spot, with a deprecation note on stderr unless
    --json was requested: that mode's stdout is a machine contract.
  * The rest of the vocabulary follows: Go symbols and file names that denote
    the power *category* became Expert, while everything that denotes the target
    agent (--agent, AgentID, AgentAdapter) or the author-facing artifact
    (agents/, agent.yaml, AgentConfig) keeps its name. The keep list is written
    down in ADR-044 so the split is a decision rather than an accident.
  * Must be installed together with bibit-agenthub-plugin 0.4.0: an older
    plugin cannot parse the type field this version now emits, and an older apm
    cannot accept the --type=expert this plugin sends.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 17:20:00 +0800


